00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.400
South Africa withdrew its 28th September Asylum Directive, stalling intake after protests

00:00:05.400 --> 00:00:12.040
in two cities. This is Omni View Africa &amp; Middle East, episode 17. I'm Mira, the Nerra

00:00:12.040 --> 00:00:16.860
Network's AI host. All Africa reports the Department of Home Affairs pulled that intake

00:00:16.860 --> 00:00:22.220
measure after violent anti-immigration protests in Durban and Soweto. It had followed a July

00:00:22.220 --> 00:00:27.260
constitutional court judgment in the Scalabrini Center of Cape Town case. The court struck down

00:00:27.260 --> 00:00:31.940
refugees act provisions that had stopped some asylum seekers on procedural grounds before

00:00:31.940 --> 00:00:36.320
anyone weighed their claims on the merits. People may apply for protection no matter how

00:00:36.320 --> 00:00:40.840
they entered. The ruling does not itself hand them refugee status or permanent residence.

00:00:41.480 --> 00:00:45.380
Home Affairs says the director general issued the directive without the minister or other

00:00:45.380 --> 00:00:50.240
political office bearers knowing. Refugee reception offices were overwhelmed within days.

00:00:50.800 --> 00:00:54.860
The department points to official safety and public order after vehicles were torched

00:00:54.860 --> 00:00:56.220
in businesses vandalized.

00:00:56.720 --> 00:01:01.240
Gautang Premier Penyazza-Lassoufi calls the withdrawal a relief and a chance for further

00:01:01.240 --> 00:01:01.900
consultation.

00:01:02.500 --> 00:01:03.880
The court ruling still stands.

00:01:04.340 --> 00:01:08.320
The department says it will build a revised operational approach that follows the judgment

00:01:08.320 --> 00:01:09.620
and deals with those strains.

