00:02:28.540 --> 00:02:32.440
The case on both sides of ending the war turns on whether Iran must cut enrichment.

00:02:33.020 --> 00:02:36.920
U.S. Vice President J.D. Vance told Reuters that Iran has to make a meaningful

00:02:36.920 --> 00:02:40.960
reduction in enrichment capacity to meet American demands and end the war.

00:02:41.480 --> 00:02:42.800
His reason is the fuel level.

00:02:43.180 --> 00:02:48.120
If Iran does not want a nuclear weapon, he asked, why does it need 60% enriched fuel,

00:02:48.480 --> 00:02:52.000
and why build highly enriched fuel if it wants to show it is not building one?

00:02:52.400 --> 00:02:53.820
He calls that a basic threshold.

00:02:54.380 --> 00:02:59.060
Washington stays open to an agreement, he said, but will not trade words for actions, and

00:02:59.060 --> 00:03:01.360
wants concrete Iranian nuclear concessions.

00:03:01.740 --> 00:03:06.740
Iranian lawmaker Al-Adan Borujerdi told Nor News that Tehran is not aiming to produce

00:03:06.740 --> 00:03:11.360
nuclear weapons and that the program is pursued under the nuclear non-proliferation treaty.

00:03:11.800 --> 00:03:16.100
His reason for refusing to treat enrichment as a concession to Washington is that those

00:03:16.100 --> 00:03:20.580
activities are unrelated to the United States and that Washington should meet its own duties

00:03:20.580 --> 00:03:21.960
on nuclear arms reduction.

00:03:22.540 --> 00:03:26.100
Both treat enrichment as the live issue and whether the war ends by agreement.

00:03:26.660 --> 00:03:28.900
Neither describes an agreement as already reached.

00:03:29.400 --> 00:03:34.120
Vance has set a cut in enrichment, including highly enriched fuel, as the price of satisfying

00:03:34.120 --> 00:03:34.700
Washington.

00:03:34.700 --> 00:03:39.540
Boro-Jurdy has rejected the claim that Iran seeks a weapon and has pointed back to the

00:03:39.540 --> 00:03:42.160
treaty into American arms reduction obligations.

00:03:42.520 --> 00:03:45.020
